# Contributing Guide

This document explains how our team works with GitHub. Please read this before pushing any code.

---

## Branch Structure

We have 3 permanent branches. **Never push directly to any of them** — always use a Pull Request.

| Branch | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `main` | Live / production. Only updated when client approves. |
| `staging` | Client preview. Updated at the end of each sprint. |
| `develop` | Team's shared working branch. Everyone merges their work here. |

---

## Your Daily Workflow

### Step 1 — Create your feature branch

Always start from `develop`. In GitHub Desktop:

1. Switch to `develop` and click **Fetch origin** to get the latest version
2. Click **Current Branch** → **New Branch**
3. Name it: `feature/yourname-taskname` (e.g. `feature/ali-tokenizer`)
4. Make sure it says **"from develop"** — very important!

### Step 2 — Do your work

Add your files to the project folder. GitHub Desktop will detect them automatically.

Use clear commit messages with these prefixes:
- `feat:` for new features (e.g. `feat: add emotion classifier`)
- `fix:` for bug fixes (e.g. `fix: correct label mapping`)
- `docs:` for documentation (e.g. `docs: update README`)
- `test:` for tests (e.g. `test: add unit tests for pipeline`)

### Step 3 — Commit and Push

1. In GitHub Desktop, write your commit message in the Summary box
2. Click **Commit to feature/yourname-taskname**
3. Click **Push origin**

### Step 4 — Open a Pull Request

1. GitHub Desktop will show **Create Pull Request** — click it
2. In the browser, make sure it says: `base: develop ← compare: feature/yourname-taskname`
3. Add a clear title and short description of what you did
4. Click **Create Pull Request**
5. Share the PR link with the team on the group chat

### Step 5 — Review and Merge

- At least **1 teammate must review and approve** your PR before it can be merged
- The reviewer clicks **Files changed** to see what was added
- If everything looks good → **Approve** → **Submit review**
- If something needs fixing → **Request changes** → write a comment
- After approval → click **Merge Pull Request** → **Confirm merge**
- The feature branch is deleted automatically after merging ✅

---

## Sprint Release Cycle

At the end of every sprint, the team lead opens a PR:

```
develop → staging
```

This deploys to Azure and the client reviews it here. **Never show the client a feature branch or develop directly.**

When the client approves:

```
staging → main
```

This is the official release.

---

## Naming Conventions

Always use lowercase and hyphens. No spaces, no capital letters, no file extensions.

```
✅ feature/ali-tokenizer
✅ feature/ceren-initial-model
✅ feature/zofia-data-preprocessing

❌ features/Ali/model.py
❌ feature/Ali_Tokenizer
❌ feature/ceren-requirements.txt
```

---

## Important Rules

- 🔒 **Never push directly** to `main`, `staging`, or `develop`
- 🌿 **Always branch from `develop`**, not from `main`
- 👀 **Always get 1 approval** before merging into `develop`
- 🗑️ **Delete your feature branch** after merging (happens automatically)
- 📝 **Use commit prefixes** — `feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `test:`
- 🔄 **Always fetch the latest `develop`** before creating a new branch

---

## Quick Reference

```
create branch from develop
       ↓
do your work + commit
       ↓
push + open PR → develop
       ↓
teammate reviews + approves
       ↓
merge + branch deleted ✅
       ↓ (end of sprint)
develop → staging → show client
       ↓ (client approves)
staging → main ✅
```
